    "doi": "https://doi.org/10.3389/frwa.2026.1702232",
    "abstract": "This study develops an integrated HEC-RAS-MODFLOW model coupled with a nonlinear evaporation module to simulate the water resource balance of the Golmud River Basin, an arid inland basin on the Tibetan Plateau, from 1990 to 2017. The model addresses limitations of previous linear evaporation approaches and isolated surface\u2013groundwater simulations by incorporating dynamic exchange processes and multi-decadal climate variability. Results indicate that river seepage constitutes 63.0% of total groundwater recharge, while groundwater levels decline at an average rate of 0.10 m/year due to combined climatic variability and anthropogenic extraction. The nonlinear evaporation module reduced simulation errors by over 15% compared to conventional linear models. Sensitivity analysis identifies hydraulic conductivity and evaporation coefficients as key parameters influencing model reliability. Under current extraction trends, groundwater discharge to terminal wetlands is projected to decrease by 50% by 2030, threatening local ecosystems. The study provides a robust modeling framework for sustainable water management in arid basins, emphasizing the need for controlled groundwater extraction, water-saving irrigation practices, and maintenance of ecological flows to mitigate aquifer depletion and wetland degradation under climate and anthropogenic pressures.",
    "field": "Water resource modeling",
    "problem": "Previous water balance evaluations for arid basins are unreliable due to inadequate models that use linear evaporation, simulate surface and groundwater in isolation, and ignore multi-decadal climate variability and dynamic exchanges between water sources.",
    "method": "This study proposes an integrated HEC-RAS-MODFLOW model coupled with a nonlinear evaporation module to simulate the water resource balance and dynamic surface-groundwater exchange processes in the Golmud River Basin.",
    "task": "The task is to simulate the water resource balance of the Golmud River Basin using an integrated HEC-RAS-MODFLOW model coupled with a nonlinear evaporation module to assess surface-groundwater interactions and long-term climate variability.",
