            "paragraph": "Stage 1: Stable Flow Simulation (Mid-1980s) during the mid-1980s, groundwater and brine extraction were minimal, accounting for less than 3% of the total recharge. Consequently, the groundwater flow field during this period can be approximated as a quasi-stable state. This stage primarily focuses on validating the mathematical model structure and the spatial distribution of hydrogeological parameters, such as the equivalent low-permeability layers and parameters related to river and spring leakage. Data from geological surveys, urban water supply investigations, and measured spring and river flow rates serve as critical constraints for establishing this stable flow model.",
            "ontology_conformed": true,
            "evidence_validated": true,
            "valid": false,
            "eval": false
        },
        {
            "source_entity_instance": "HEC-RAS-MODFLOW model",
            "source_entity_type": "Source",
            "relation": "HAS_SIMULATION_TYPE",
            "target_entity_instance": "Non-Steady Process Simulation",
            "target_entity_type": "SimulationType",
            "paragraph": "Stage 2: Non-Steady Process Simulation (1985\u20132000) Using the stable flow field from the mid-1980s as the initial condition, a non-steady groundwater flow model was established for the period from 1985 to 2000. This stage utilizes long-term dynamic monitoring data from over ten observation wells and annual fluctuations in groundwater levels to identify parameters such as aquifer recharge rates and storage coefficients. During calibration, measured data\u2014including groundwater extraction rates, spring discharge rates, and river leakage/discharge flux\u2014are used to constrain the model and improve its fit to the prototype.",
            "ontology_conformed": true,
            "evidence_validated": true,
